<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roanoke River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roanoke River rises in the Blue Ridge Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and flows south-east into North Carolina, where it reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albemarle-sound">Albemarle Sound</a>. Its lower valley was home to northern Tuscarora towns, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tom-blount">Tom Blount</a> stayed out of the war of 1711. The river shares its name with Roanoke Island, site of the lost English colony of the 1580s, and with the city of Roanoke in Virginia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roanoke-river.jpg" alt="Roanoke River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patriarca12 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roanoke_River_in_Wasena,_Roanoke,_Virginia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Roanoke River is 410 miles (660 km) long and drains a largely rural area from the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albemarle-sound">Albemarle Sound</a>. It begins at Lafayette in Montgomery County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, where the North Fork and South Fork meet, and flows through the Roanoke Valley and past the city of Roanoke.</p><p>It then crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and enters North Carolina north of Roanoke Rapids at the fall line, before zigzagging across the coastal plain to Batchelor Bay. An 81-mile stretch of its lower course in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, between Leesville Lake and Kerr Lake, is known as the Staunton River. The river is impounded in six places, among them Smith Mountain Lake, Kerr Lake and Lake Gaston.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The valley was the homeland of Native peoples such as the Moratoc, Occaneechi, Tuscarora and Tutelo, and its deadly spring floods earned it the name River of Death. Virginians began settling its lower course about the middle of the 17th century, in what were called the Albemarle Settlements.</p><p>Fur trading parties sent by Abraham Wood explored its upper reaches in the late 17th century, but the English did not settle them until the early 18th century. In 1883 the town of Big Lick was chosen as a major shops and terminal point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and Western Railway and was renamed Roanoke after the river, as Roanoke County had been in 1838.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river flooded severely until the John H. Kerr Dam was completed in 1953, a project prompted by the record floods of the 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> hurricane. The Niagara Dam near Roanoke, built in 1906 to power the city's streetcars, is the first of its dams. A paddle trail, the Roanoke River State Trail, follows the lower river from Roanoke Rapids to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albemarle-sound">Albemarle Sound</a>. It was developed from 1997 by the non-profit Roanoke River Partners and became North Carolina's twelfth state trail by a law passed on 18 November 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roanoke River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tuscarora lived on the coastal plain of North Carolina along the <b>Roanoke</b>, Tar and Neuse rivers, an Iroquoian-speaking people among Algonquian and Siouan neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
